Class loading.

Your program is a pile of dot-class files, but the java virtual machine starts with almost none of them in memory. Classes are pulled in lazily — on first use — by objects called class loaders. This is the machinery that turns a dot-class file on disk into a live, usable class inside the running virtual machine. And it's what makes plugins, application servers, and hot-reload possible.

A class isn't loaded when the program starts; it's loaded the first time something needs it — the first new, the first static access. And bringing a class in happens in three phases. First, loading: find the dot-class bytes, whether from disk, a jar, or the network, and parse them into an in-memory representation, producing a class object. Second, linking, which itself has three steps — verify that the bytecode is well-formed and safe, prepare the static fields with default values, and resolve symbolic references to other classes. And third, initialization: run the class's static initializers and static field assignments, top to bottom. That's the moment your static blocks actually execute. So if a class has a static field computed by a method call, and a static block that prints a message, both run at initialization — which is on first use of that class, not at startup. That laziness is why a static block can seem to fire late — it fires exactly when the class is first touched.

The verification step deserves a moment, because it's a quiet but crucial guarantee. Before any bytecode runs, the verifier proves it can't corrupt the virtual machine — no stack underflow, no jumping to a bad address, no treating an integer as an object reference. That's why untrusted bytecode can be run at all: a malformed or hostile dot-class is rejected at load time, not left to crash the machine mid-run.

Now, class loaders form a parent-child hierarchy, and they cooperate by delegation. When a loader is asked for a class, it first asks its parent, and only loads the class itself if the parent can't. The chain, from the top down, is: the bootstrap loader, which loads the core java-dot-lang classes and is part of the virtual machine itself; the platform loader, which loads other standard modules; and the application, or system, loader, which loads your classes from the classpath. This parent-first delegation means core types, like java-dot-lang-dot-String, are always loaded by the bootstrap loader — so no application code can shadow them with a counterfeit. That's a security and consistency property.

Here's a subtle rule with big consequences: a class's identity is its name plus the loader that defined it. The same dot-class loaded by two different loaders becomes two distinct types — an object of one is not assignable to the other, and comparing them throws a class-cast exception. That sounds like a nuisance, but it's exactly the feature that powers real systems. Application servers give each deployed app its own loader, so two apps can use different versions of the same library without collision. Plugin systems load and unload modules at runtime by loading them in separate loaders. And hot reload, in your dev tools, works by discarding a loader and re-loading the changed classes in a fresh one. So class loading isn't just a startup detail — it's an extensibility mechanism. Most of the time it's invisible; when you build plugins, or run inside a container, it's the model you reach for. Next, we look at how Java formalized code boundaries at a larger scale than the class: modules.
